 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T12:59+08:00",
 "answer": "Yes, the haze situation in Singapore is **bad**. \n\nTransboundary smoke haze driven by fires in Kalimantan and Sumatra has heavily affected the region. Air quality is sitting firmly in the **Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy** range, with the National Environment Agency (NEA) forecasting 24-hour PSI levels between **150 and 230**. \n\n**Key details to keep in mind:**\n* **Health Impact:** At these levels, the air quality can cause irritation to the eyes, nose, and throat for healthy individuals. \n* **Advisories:** \n  * **General public / Healthy individuals:** Should avoid prolonged or strenuous outdoor physical exertion.\n  * **Elderly, pregnant women, children, and those with chronic heart or lung conditions:** Should minimize or entirely avoid outdoor activities, and keep medications or masks handy if they need to step out.\n\nIt is recommended to check real-time updates via the **myENV app** or [haze.gov.sg](https://www.haze.gov.sg) for the latest 1-hour PM2.5 readings before heading outdoors.",
